    {
      "claim": "the $h_K=1$ Lichnerowicz space is one-dimensional, so $\\lambda_1$ vanishes by construction (the only eigenvalue is the constant mode).",
      "issue": "Incorrect. The modular curve $X_0(67)$ has infinite-dimensional $L^2$ spectrum; the Laplacian has continuous spectrum and typically many discrete eigenvalues besides the constant mode. The claim that the only eigenvalue is the constant mode is false, invalidating the falsification of candidate $P_3$.",
      "line_hint": "In the paragraph explaining the Lichnerowicz test, after Table I."
    },
    {
      "claim": "entropy $= 0$ at $h_K=1$",
      "issue": "The Bekenstein--Hawking entropy $S_{\\mathrm{BH}}(K)$ is not defined in the paper; it is unclear how a gravitational entropy formula gives zero for a number field with class number 1. Without a precise definition, the falsification is not substantiated.",
      "line_hint": "In the discussion of $P_4$."
    }
  ],
  "equation_issues": [],
  "scope_overclaims": [
    {
      "claim": "The theorems clarify \\emph{where} arithmetic data can and cannot be expected to constrain non-perturbative gauge dynamics.",
      "actual_scope": "Theorems 2 and 3 only test specific candidate identifications (five periods for $\\sqrt{\\sigma}$ and one ladder), not all possible arithmetic constructions. Future proposals could evade these negative results. Theorems 1 and 4 are essentially dimensional analysis and RG interpretations, not strong no-go statements against all arithmetic determination of the absolute scale if an extra dimensionful constant is supplied from physics. The word 'cannot' overstates the conclusiveness."
    }
  ],
  "missing_citations": [
    {
      "claim": "Lichnerowicz spectral edge for imaginary quadratic fields and its relation to Maass cusp forms on $\\Gamma_0(|D|)$ is used without definition.",
